
Notes on the ST Nucleo-H563ZI Board
-----------------------------------

https://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/nucleo-h563zi.html

Nucleo-144 form factor board built around an STM32H563ZIT6: a Cortex-M33
(ARMv8-M, single precision FPU) with 2MB of flash and 640KB of SRAM. An
ST-LINK V3EC is on board, so no external debugger is needed.

Build and run with:

    make nucleo-h563zi-test


LK Debug/Console UART
---------------------
- USART3 is used, at 115200 8N1
- TX is D8
- RX is D9
- both run to the on board ST-LINK's virtual com port, which shows up on the
  host as something like /dev/ttyACM0. No wiring needed.


LEDs and button
---------------
- LD1 (green)  is B0
- LD2 (yellow) is F4
- LD3 (red)    is G4
- the user button is C13, and reads 1 while held

The target is built with TARGET_HAS_DEBUG_LED, which means the kernel drives
the first two of those itself and they are not free for general use: LED0 (LD1)
follows whether a non-idle thread is running, out of thread.c's context switch,
and LED1 (LD2) is toggled around interrupt handling by arch/arm/arm-m/arch.c,
so it sits faintly lit whenever interrupts are busy. LD3 is untouched.


Loading with probe-rs
---------------------
probe-rs is the easy path here, and it needs no config files. Note that
upstream OpenOCD has no STM32H5 support at all (only ST's fork does), so
probe-rs is the practical choice for this board.

    probe-rs download --chip STM32H563ZI build-nucleo-h563zi-test/lk.elf
    probe-rs reset --chip STM32H563ZI

Then attach to the console with any terminal program, e.g.

    screen /dev/ttyACM0 115200

Other things worth knowing:

    probe-rs list                       # check the probe is seen at all
    probe-rs info --chip STM32H563ZI    # read the debug ports and core out
    probe-rs attach --chip STM32H563ZI build-nucleo-h563zi-test/lk.elf
    probe-rs gdb --chip STM32H563ZI     # gdb server on :1337

If probe-rs reports no probes found while the board is plugged in and
enumerating (0483:374e in lsusb), the host udev rules are probably missing or
too old; see https://probe.rs/docs/getting-started/probe-setup/. Hand written
ST-LINK rule sets predating the V3 are common, and they do not match the V3EC
on this board.


Clocking
--------
The platform currently runs from the reset HSI, which is 32MHz because the
part comes out of reset with HSIDIV set to 2. The systick rate and the uart
divisor are both derived from SystemCoreClock, so they follow whatever the
clock setup leaves behind. The board can feed 8MHz into HSE from the ST-LINK
MCO, and the part will run to 250MHz, but neither is set up yet.


TrustZone
---------
The H563 has TrustZone, but LK does not use it: the build runs in secure state
with TZEN left at its factory setting and no SAU configuration. The
securefault decoding in arch/arm/arm-m/exceptions.c is compiled out, since it
is gated on __ARM_FEATURE_CMSE == 3 and nothing passes -mcmse. If a board has
been provisioned with TZEN enabled at some point, expect it not to boot as is;
check the option bytes with STM32CubeProgrammer.
